---
import Refugios from "../../../layouts/Refugios.astro";

/**
 * Too many code requests from one place.
 *
 * A page of its own rather than a reuse of `espera.astro`, which is where this refusal first
 * landed — and that was wrong in a way worth recording. The ceiling page says "hoy ya se
 * llegó al tope" and "no es nada que hayas hecho tú", and **both sentences are false here**:
 * the platform's mail budget is untouched, and the caller's own request rate is precisely
 * what happened. Telling a shelter that the platform is out of mail when it is not is the
 * kind of lie that turns a five-minute wait into a support conversation.
 *
 * It reveals nothing about any address. The per-IP limit counts requests from one caller,
 * whatever they were for, so a registered and an unregistered address reach this page under
 * exactly the same conditions — the same property that lets `espera.astro` exist alongside
 * ADR 0008's identical-response rule.
 *
 * Being told you are rate-limited is not a leak either: the caller already knows how many
 * requests it made. What it must not learn is which *addresses* exist, and this says nothing
 * about that.
 */
---

<Refugios title="Demasiados intentos" heading="Demasiados intentos desde aquí">
  <p data-testid="ip-limit">
    Se pidieron muchos códigos desde esta conexión en poco tiempo, así que hay que esperar un
    rato antes del próximo.
  </p>

  <p data-testid="try-later">
    Vuelve a intentarlo <strong>en una hora</strong>. Si no fuiste tú, puede ser que estén
    compartiendo la conexión — pasa en un cibercafé o con el internet de un edificio.
  </p>

  <p>
    <!--
      Said because the shelter's natural fear on reading this is that it has been blocked.
      The limit is on requests from one connection in one hour, and it forgets: nothing about
      the refugio changes, and no code it already received stops working.
    -->
    Esto no bloquea tu refugio ni anula un código que ya te haya llegado.
  </p>

  <p><a href="/refugios/entrar">Volver</a></p>
</Refugios>
